 has gone on and  initially with cable and very  specialized channels and now with  streaming and algorithms micro targeting  certain demographics  yeah tv can get really niche  and as a result  it actually is worth taking more chances  because if something like  netflix or hulu or whatever  wants to make a very queer friendly show  the algorithm is going to help ensure  that that is really only put in front of  the eyes of queer audiences like some  conservative people will still find out  it exists and get annoyed but it's not  the same as like back in the 80s or 90s  when major networks started putting any  of that kind of material on there  because that was being seen by people  who were definitely not okay with it  that was guaranteed that it was going to  be seen by people who did not want to  see it so it was a bigger risk to do  back then  not so much now  there's other reasons that tv tends to  be more progressive and you get more not  only instances of representation in tv  and streaming but you also get way more  queer creators you get much deeper  characterizations way less tokenism  and a big part of that has to do with  the fact that there's less money  involved in tv and streaming not  necessarily collectively but on a  per-project basis it costs less it makes  you less but it also costs less the cost  of a season worth of television is  not that much especially compared to  something like a true proper hollywood  big budget movie and part of the things  that help mitigate that is with  television you don't need necessarily a  big name star  or a big name director you can  swing a bat with a smaller budget on  more out there ideas or new voices or  actors who are unknown and are also  consequently significantly cheaper again  it's that sort of uh risk assessment  there's less financial risk involved in  television and plus there is so much  content constantly demanded by streaming  services especially at this point that  you are never ever going to co