  to put in representation and then cut it  out for the chinese release  well that does tend to result in very  token and disposable representation  things like  single scene instances or blink or  you'll miss it things between characters  who i'm not even sure they even have  names  and that is  token  i'm not going to say that's completely  meaningless i am the kind of person who  would argue i would rather have that  than nothing  like  people will say like well you're never  happy well like look  when that's what i'm getting  you're basically giving me expired gruel  but if i'm starving i'll take the  expired gruel but i am going to complain  about the fact that it's expired gruel  so  you know  it is kind of a situation where i do  think something even token removable  representation is better than nothing  especially at the point we are in this  country where there is such a fervor  over queerness trans people especially  that normalizing  queerness it it's  it's really becoming uh  a need a societal societal need but i  won't dig too far into that because  that's getting into the morals and as i  said companies don't have them so  heck with that and now even beyond china  with the international market like the  international market does matter don't  let anyone tell you otherwise however it  is worth pointing out that most studios  tend to make less on the international  market even if the international box  office is more because a lot of places  outside the u.s they either have to take  a smaller percentage as dictated by the  government as in the case of a place  like china or they have to work with  local distributors who take a cut and so  there is less of a cut to be able to be  taken by the studio releasing it  basically the u.s still tends to be the  single most important market not only  because a it's the biggest single market  but b  studios tend to get the biggest piece of  the pie there but international markets  do matter and the chinese market 